# Nidara identifies itself as Nidara and says, in the field that exists for
# exactly that, that it IS an Arch system. Both halves matter: the name is ours
# (rebrand), the lineage is Arch's and stated as such (no appropriation). Arch's
# name and logo are trademarks; "based on Arch Linux" is nominative use and
# ID_LIKE="arch" is the same statement in a form scripts can read.
#
# This file is the LIVE medium's alone, and it is deliberately not a package.
#
# The installed system gets its identity from `nidara-release`, which OWNS
# /etc/os-release so a correction can travel by `pacman -Syu`. That package lives
# in nidara-repo (it was in ../../../packages/ until 2026-08-30) and is NOT on
# this image: an airootfs overlay would overwrite the file it owns and leave
# `pacman -Qkk` calling the medium tampered with for as long as it runs.
#
# So the two files differ on purpose, in one way that matters: PRETTY_NAME says
# "(live)", which is worth being able to see in a bug report. Neither carries a
# VERSION — the machine is rolling and `BUILD_ID` is the whole answer (PRODUCT.md,
# "The machine is rolling, the image has a date"). This one used to explain its
# missing VERSION by pointing at a ../../../VERSION file; that file is gone, and
# there is no number to point at any more.
#
# ⚠️ The image's date is NOT written here and must not be: `mkarchiso` appends
# `IMAGE_ID` and `IMAGE_VERSION` to this file at build time, and IMAGE_VERSION is
# `iso_version` from profiledef.sh — the build date. A hand-written copy would be
# a second answer to a question archiso already answers.
NAME="Nidara"
PRETTY_NAME="Nidara (live)"
ID=nidara
ID_LIKE="arch"
BUILD_ID=rolling
ANSI_COLOR="0;36"
HOME_URL="https://github.com/nidara-project"
DOCUMENTATION_URL="https://github.com/nidara-project/nidara-desktop"
SUPPORT_URL="https://github.com/nidara-project/nidara-desktop/issues"
BUG_REPORT_URL="https://github.com/nidara-project/nidara-desktop/issues"
LOGO=nidara
